---
import StudioLayout from '../layouts/StudioLayout.astro';
import '../styles/training.css';
import {
	bookHref, isCalendar, CONTACT_EMAIL, PORTFOLIO_URL, LINKEDIN_URL,
	proof, showcase, workFacts, workLead, prototypes, capabilities,
	games, myGames, services, offers, designRules, learnerOutcomes, whyUs, steps, forList, notForList, faqs, PLAYABLE_ANCHOR,
} from '../data/training';

const external = isCalendar() ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener' } : {};
const demo = bookHref('Training and simulation project');
const demoLabel = isCalendar() ? 'Book a 20-minute demo' : 'Discuss your project';
const pitch = bookHref('A minigame for our platform');
const portfolioShowcase = showcase.filter((item) => item.id === 'tools');
const artClass: Record<string, string> = { range: '', minigames: 'lilac', tools: 'peach' };
---
<StudioLayout
	title="Cyber training games & product design | Strategic Sloth"
	description="Strategic Sloth designs and builds educational cyber minigames and training interfaces for cyber ranges and security training platforms. Play one and see the rest."
>
<div class="tr">

<div class="tr-stage">
<section class="container tr-pitch" aria-labelledby="tr-title">
	<p class="eyebrow">STRATEGIC SLOTH · CYBER TRAINING GAMES &amp; PRODUCT DESIGN</p>
	<h1 id="tr-title">Cyber training that plays like a game.</h1>
	<div class="tr-pitch-row">
		<p class="lede">We design and build educational minigames, and the interfaces around them, for cyber ranges and security training platforms. Eight games of our own so far. One is playable right here.</p>
		<div class="hero-actions">
			<a class="button" href={demo} {...external}>{demoLabel} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
			<a class="text-link" href="#our-games">See the games <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="container" aria-labelledby="featured-title">
	<div class="tr-featured">
		<a class="tr-featured-art" href={PLAYABLE_ANCHOR} aria-label="Play Keep Finance Online">
			<img src="/training/work/network-defense-game.webp" alt="Keep Finance Online gameplay: an isometric network with a gateway, an inspection point, a core server, and a Finance connection to restore." width="1600" height="1000" fetchpriority="high" />
			<span class="tr-featured-signal" aria-hidden="true"><i>●</i> FINANCE OFFLINE · YOUR MOVE</span>
		</a>
		<div class="tr-featured-copy">
			<p class="eyebrow">FEATURED GAME · PLAYABLE NOW</p>
			<h2 id="featured-title">Keep Finance Online</h2>
			<p>Finance is down and traffic is coming in. Inspect it, write the access rules, and bring the connection back without letting a threat through.</p>
			<a class="button" href={PLAYABLE_ANCHOR}><span class="tr-play-icon" aria-hidden="true">▶</span> Play it now</a>
			<small>3 rounds · About 5 minutes · No sign-up</small>
		</div>
	</div>
	<div class="tr-proof">
		{proof.map((p) => <div><strong>{p.title}</strong><span>{p.detail}</span></div>)}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="container tr-games-section" id="our-games" aria-labelledby="our-games-title">
	<div class="section-heading">
		<div><p class="eyebrow">OUR GAMES · DESIGNED AND BUILT BY US</p><h2 id="our-games-title">Eight training games, playable from briefing to debrief.</h2></div>
		<p>Each clip shows a real decision, one mistake, the game’s own explanation, and the fix. Every game has authored scenarios and a test suite that proves the lazy strategy loses.</p>
	</div>
	<div class="tr-gamegrid">
		{myGames.map((g) => (
			<article class="tr-gcard">
				<div class="tr-gcard-art">
					<img src={`/training/my-games/${g.id}.jpg`} alt={`${g.name} gameplay`} width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" />
					<video class="tr-gcard-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"><source data-src={`/training/my-games/${g.id}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" /></video>
					<span class="tr-gcard-genre">{g.genre}</span>
					<span class="tr-gcard-live" aria-hidden="true">● Gameplay</span>
					<h3>{g.name.split(':')[0]}</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="tr-gcard-info">
					<strong>Teaches: {g.teaches}</strong>
					<p class="tr-gcard-why"><b>After a mistake, the game says:</b> {g.mistake}</p>
					<p><b>Proved by tests.</b> {g.proof}</p>
					<p>{g.fits}</p>
				</div>
			</article>
		))}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="container tr-games-section" id="games" aria-labelledby="games-title">
	<div class="section-heading">
		<div><p class="eyebrow">ALSO FEATURED · FROM A COMMERCIAL CYBER RANGE</p><h2 id="games-title">One skill. A few minutes. A score.</h2></div>
		<p>Six of these were built for a commercial cyber range and are shown here under neutral names. Each one teaches a single skill a learner can practice on a coffee break.</p>
	</div>
	<div class="tr-gamegrid">
		{games.map((g) => (
			<article class="tr-gcard">
				<button class="tr-gcard-art" type="button" data-case={g.id} aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-label={`${g.name}: watch gameplay and read the design thinking`}>
					<img src={`/training/games-art/${g.id}-720.webp`} srcset={`/training/games-art/${g.id}-720.webp 720w, /training/games-art/${g.id}-1280.webp 1280w`} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 640px" alt={g.alt} width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" />
					<video class="tr-gcard-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"><source data-src={`/training/games-video/${g.id}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" /></video>
					<span class="tr-gcard-genre">{g.genre}</span>
					<span class="tr-gcard-live" aria-hidden="true">● Gameplay</span>
					<h3>{g.name}</h3>
				</button>
				<div class="tr-gcard-info">
					<strong>Teaches: {g.teaches}</strong>
					<p>{g.body}</p>
					<p class="tr-gcard-why"><b>Why it works:</b> {g.why}</p>
					<button class="tr-gcard-more" type="button" data-case={g.id} aria-haspopup="dialog">See the design thinking <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></button>
				</div>
			</article>
		))}
		<article class="tr-gcard">
			<a class="tr-gcard-art" href={PLAYABLE_ANCHOR}>
				<img src="/training/work/network-defense-game.webp" alt="" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
				<span class="tr-gcard-genre">Strategy · Playable now</span>
				<h3>Keep Finance Online</h3>
			</a>
			<div class="tr-gcard-info">
				<strong>Teaches: Firewall rules and service availability</strong>
				<p>Our own network defense game. Inspect traffic, scope the rules, add a backup route, then replay every decision in the debrief. <a href={PLAYABLE_ANCHOR}>Play it below ↓</a></p>
			</div>
		</article>
		<article class="tr-gcard tr-gcard-yours">
			<a class="tr-gcard-art" href={pitch} {...external}>
				<span class="tr-gcard-genre">Next up</span>
				<h3>Your game here</h3>
				<span class="tr-gcard-plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
			</a>
			<div class="tr-gcard-info">
				<strong>Teaches: The skill your learners keep failing</strong>
				<p>Tell us the learning objective. We come back with game concepts sized to your platform and your content. <a href={pitch} {...external}>Pitch us a skill ↗</a></p>
			</div>
		</article>
	</div>
</section>
</div>

{games.map((g) => (
	<dialog class="tr-case" id={`case-${g.id}`} aria-labelledby={`case-title-${g.id}`}>
		<div class="tr-case-inner">
			<button class="tr-case-close" type="button" data-case-close aria-label="Close">×</button>
			<div class="tr-case-media">
				<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster={`/training/games-video/${g.id}.jpg`}><source data-src={`/training/games-video/${g.id}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" /></video>
				<p>Real gameplay from the working build, shown under a neutral name.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="tr-case-copy">
				<p class="eyebrow">{g.genre.toUpperCase()} · DESIGN CASE STUDY</p>
				<h2 id={`case-title-${g.id}`}>{g.name}</h2>
				<p class="tr-case-teaches">Teaches: {g.teaches}</p>
				<dl>
					<div><dt>The problem</dt><dd>{g.problem}</dd></div>
					<div><dt>The idea</dt><dd>{g.idea}</dd></div>
					<div><dt>UX decisions</dt><dd><ul>{g.ux.map((u) => <li>{u}</li>)}</ul></dd></div>
					<div><dt>What the learner gets</dt><dd>{g.learner}</dd></div>
				</dl>
				<a class="button" href={bookHref(`A game like ${g.name}`)} {...external}>Build one like this <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</dialog>
))}

<section class="section container compact" id="minigame-example" aria-labelledby="minigame-title">
	<div class="section-heading">
		<div><p class="eyebrow">PLAY ONE NOW</p><h2 id="minigame-title">Keep Finance Online</h2></div>
		<p>Do not take our word for it. Play three rounds, then picture it inside your course.</p>
	</div>
	<div class="tr-playable">
		<div class="tr-playable-intro" id="game-intro">
			<div class="tr-game-preview">
				<img src="/training/work/network-defense-game.webp" alt="Network Defense gameplay: an isometric network with a gateway, inspection point, core server, and Finance connection to restore." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
				<span class="tr-preview-badge">ACTUAL GAMEPLAY</span>
				<div class="tr-preview-signal" aria-hidden="true"><span>●</span> FINANCE OFFLINE · YOUR MOVE</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tr-game-invitation">
			<span class="status launch">Playable prototype · About 5 minutes</span>
			<h3>The network is yours.<br />Keep it online.</h3>
			<p>Finance is offline. Traffic is coming in. Can you restore the connection without letting a threat through?</p>
			<button class="button" type="button" id="play-network-defense"><span class="tr-play-icon" aria-hidden="true">▶</span> Play the example</button>
			<small class="tr-game-note">3 rounds · No sign-up · Play in your browser</small>
			<div class="tr-preview-actions" role="group" aria-label="Explore the game mechanics">
				<button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-preview-detail="Inspect traffic to uncover evidence. You decide what belongs on the network.">01 Inspect</button>
				<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-preview-detail="Configure access rules to stop prohibited activity while keeping essential services available.">02 Protect</button>
				<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-preview-detail="Build a backup route when a link fails, then replay your decisions in the debrief.">03 Recover</button>
			</div>
			<p class="tr-preview-detail" aria-live="polite">Inspect traffic to uncover evidence. You decide what belongs on the network.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<iframe id="network-defense-game" title="Keep Finance Online playable example" data-game-src="/training/games/network-defense/index.html" allow="fullscreen" hidden></iframe>
		<p class="tr-demo-loading" role="status" hidden>Loading the game…</p>
	</div>
	<div class="tr-proto-meta">
		<div><strong>Keep Finance Online</strong><span>Beginner cybersecurity practice · Three rounds · Decision replay and debrief</span></div>
		<a class="text-link" href="/training/games/network-defense/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full screen <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section container compact tr-mc" id="mission-control" aria-labelledby="mc-title">
<div class="cg-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">CASE STUDY / MISSION CONTROL PROTOTYPE</p><h2 id="mc-title">Mission Control: one screen that always answers “what do I do next?”</h2></div><p>One of our early prototypes: the home screen of a gamified cyber training platform. We designed it and built it as a working front end, so every interaction below can be tried, not just described.</p></div>
<div class="mc-hero">
  <video controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster="/training/mission-control/mission-control.jpg" aria-label="Walkthrough of the Mission Control prototype"><source src="/training/mission-control/mission-control.mp4" type="video/mp4"><a href="/training/mission-control/mission-control.mp4">Watch the walkthrough</a></video>
</div>
<p class="cg-note">21-second walkthrough · Press play to load · <a href="/training/mission-map/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Open the live prototype ↗</b></a> (desktop, sample data)</p>
<div class="cg-facts cg-facts-tight mc-meta">
<div><h3>The design question</h3><p>A learner logs in with twenty minutes to spare. How do they see where they are, pick the next mission, and start it, without reading a manual or getting lost in features?</p></div>
<div><h3>Our role</h3><p>UX and UI design, game systems design, and the front-end prototype. Screens, states, motion, and copy are ours, along with the design specs for ranks, level-ups, and the debrief.</p></div>
<div><h3>How it is built</h3><p>React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind. The map is plain DOM with CSS 3D: four layers at different depths give parallax as the camera moves, with no canvas or game engine.</p></div>
</div>

<h3 class="mc-part">The UX thinking</h3>

<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/01-mission-control-default.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/01-mission-control-default.webp" alt="Mission control map with the mission list on the left, numbered markers on a world map, and the current objective card at lower right." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UX 01 / HIERARCHY</p><h3>Three layers: what to do, who you are, what is happening.</h3><p>A mission hub can drown a learner in status. We sorted every element into one of three layers and gave each a fixed place, so the eye learns the screen once.</p><ul><li><b>Primary:</b> the mission list, the numbered markers on the map, and the current objective card with its one button.</li><li><b>Secondary:</b> identity and level, the active operation, and the control to change learning path.</li><li><b>Ambient:</b> the live ticker, global events, satellites, and vessels. They make the world feel alive and never ask for a decision.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row mc-flip">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/03-tutorial-step2-mission-list-spotlight.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/03-tutorial-step2-mission-list-spotlight.webp" alt="Tutorial step: the screen is dimmed, the first mission is ringed, and a popover explains the mission list." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UX 02 / ONBOARDING</p><h3>Say the one thing that matters, then get out of the way.</h3><p>The first-run tutorial is three steps. It tells the learner that the learning path is the main mission and everything else on the map is optional, then spotlights the first mission and the button that starts it.</p><ul><li>The learner completes each step by doing it, not by pressing Next. A wrong click shakes the hint instead of blocking.</li><li>The map stays live behind the dimmed overlay, so the tutorial teaches the real screen, not a slideshow.</li><li>A longer eight-step tour is available on demand for learners who want it.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/05-mission-selected-marker-tooltip.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/05-mission-selected-marker-tooltip.webp" alt="A mission selected in the list, its marker tooltip open on the map, and the objective card updated to match." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UX 03 / THE CALL TO ACTION</p><h3>One button that always tells the truth.</h3><p>Starting a mission spins up a live environment, which takes time and can fail. We put that whole process into a single button whose label is the state, so there is never a second place to look.</p><ul><li>Idle, loading, ready, continue: four labels on the same control, with progress shown inside it.</li><li>A long press during loading aborts, with a fill bar that shows the hold. Rapid repeat clicks are caught and explained.</li><li>Locked missions say why they are locked. Hovering a mission eases the camera to its marker and cross-fades the objective card.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row mc-flip">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/08-learning-path-selector.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/08-learning-path-selector.webp" alt="Learning path selector sheet with category filters and a row of path cards, one highlighted with an Initialize Path button." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UX 04 / CHOOSING A PATH</p><h3>Compare twenty paths without leaving the map.</h3><p>Changing learning path is a big decision that should feel reversible. The selector opens as a sheet over the map, with a full-screen grid for people who want to browse.</p><ul><li>Filter by category, sort by name, move with arrow keys, close with Escape.</li><li>Each card shows duration and last activity before any commitment.</li><li>A confirm step says exactly what will change and that current progress is saved.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/07-mission-console.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/07-mission-console.webp" alt="Mission console with a learning modules rail on the left and a terminal showing packet capture analysis." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UX 05 / INTO THE WORK</p><h3>From the map to the keyboard in two clicks.</h3><p>The mission console keeps the task, the progress, and the terminal on one screen, so the learner never tabs away to remember what they were asked.</p><ul><li>Learning modules and overall progress on a left rail, the answer box and hint under it.</li><li>The terminal takes the rest of the screen. The world ticker stays at the bottom for continuity with the hub.</li><li>The console spec covers four exercise types, a timer with a five-minute warning, and hints that cost points.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<h3 class="mc-part">The game design thinking</h3>

<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/18-mission-results-xp-rankup-levelup.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/18-mission-results-xp-rankup-levelup.webp" alt="Results cards: player level, a work-role XP breakdown across seven roles, a rank-up strip, and a level-up banner from 5 to 6." width="1600" height="767" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">GAME DESIGN 01 / PROGRESSION</p><h3>Two tracks: how far you have come, and what you are good at.</h3><p>A single XP bar tells a security team nothing. We split progress into a player level for motivation and work-role XP for meaning, so one mission can advance a learner as a Cyber Defense Analyst and as an Incident Responder at the same time.</p><ul><li>Ten player levels on a rising XP curve, from 1,000 XP for level 2 to 27,000 for level 10.</li><li>Work-role XP is shown as a breakdown per role, with rank-ups listed only when a rank actually changed.</li><li>Celebrations are additive, not disruptive: inline, about three seconds, skippable, and never a full-screen takeover.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row mc-flip">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/spec-rank-progression.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/spec-rank-progression.webp" alt="Rank progression chart showing tiers of helmet icons in cyan, bronze, silver, and gold with star counts." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><span class="mc-tag">Design spec</span></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">GAME DESIGN 02 / RANK LADDER</p><h3>Thirty-four ranks a learner can read at a glance.</h3><p>Long ladders fail when rank 17 looks like rank 18. Each of the eight tiers gets a new helmet silhouette, each rank inside a tier steps through four metals, and stars count position, so shape, color, and number all say the same thing.</p><ul><li>Eight tiers, from Initiate to Legendary.</li><li>Four metals inside each tier: cyan, bronze, silver, gold.</li><li>Readable at avatar size, which is where a rank is seen most.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/14-global-operation-briefing.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/14-global-operation-briefing.webp" alt="An operation briefing panel on the map with severity, narrative, threat actor, sectors, regions, and days remaining." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">GAME DESIGN 03 / A LIVING WORLD</p><h3>Give people a reason to look at the map again tomorrow.</h3><p>A static hub gets ignored after the first week. Timed operations, global events, and a live feed make the map change between visits, and each one leads back to training.</p><ul><li>A weekly operation with a narrative, a threat actor, affected sectors, and a countdown, linked to related missions.</li><li>Event cards arrive a few at a time, dismiss themselves, and can be switched off for learners who want quiet.</li><li>A scrolling feed with severity chips that pauses on hover and expands on click or keyboard.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row mc-flip">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/17-code-matrix-minigame.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/17-code-matrix-minigame.webp" alt="A pattern-matching minigame: a five by five grid of hex values with a target pattern and a trace-risk bar." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">GAME DESIGN 04 / OPTIONAL PLAY</p><h3>Side games that reward curiosity without derailing the course.</h3><p>Exploration is labelled as exploration. Quick games live on the map and in the feed for learners who want a break that still exercises a skill, and they pay small XP so they never beat doing the mission.</p><ul><li>Minigame markers sit on the map beside mission markers, visually distinct.</li><li>Three quick interactions built into the world: a pattern-matching grid, a signal race, and a rhythm challenge with three difficulty levels.</li><li>Rewards are small by design, about 20 to 80 XP per win.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<article class="mc-row">
  <div class="mc-row-media"><a class="mc-shot" href="/training/mission-control/spec-mission-debrief.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/training/mission-control/spec-mission-debrief.webp" alt="Mission debrief with a grade, duration bands, points deducted, bonus points, and progression cards." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><span class="mc-tag">Design spec</span></div>
  <div class="mc-row-copy"><p class="eyebrow">GAME DESIGN 05 / DEBRIEF</p><h3>Explain the score, or the score teaches nothing.</h3><p>The debrief shows the grade and then accounts for it. Learners see what they lost and what they earned, in plain categories, with a clear way to try again.</p><ul><li>A letter grade with four time bands, from optimal to failed.</li><li>Points deducted for hints, wrong attempts, and time. Bonus points for first-try accuracy, no hints, and streaks.</li><li>Three exits: leave, retry, or play the next mission. A fail still awards partial XP and points to review material.</li></ul></div>
</article>
<h3 class="mc-part">More screens</h3>
